== Introduction

Internal controls are the security practices and activities your team performs throughout the development lifecycle. They define *what* you do to build securely — from code reviews and dependency checks to release verification and incident response processes.

This register organises controls by development phase so you can see which practices apply at each stage. Use it to track adoption, identify gaps, and demonstrate to stakeholders that security is built into your process.

Internal controls by development phase:

* *Foundation* — Assign security responsibilities and ensure that the team has the competence to act on them.
* *Design* — Establish the security context, model threats and risks, and derive security requirements.
* *Implementation* — Find security defects as code is written, and document what is being built.
* *Verification* — Conduct security testing and validate that requirements are met.
* *Releases* — Pass the release security gate, protect release integrity, and document the release.
* *Operations* — Track third-party vulnerabilities and updates, and keep environments protected.

== Process scoping

Process scoping refers to deciding which internal controls are applicable for the development project.

